  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1249 PM CDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nHigh pressure at the surface will dominate the weather pattern\nthe rest of today and tonight before the center slides off to\nthe southeast on Thursday. A broad upper-level ridge remains in\nplace over the western CONUS and the axis will gradually move \ntowards the northern Plains Thursday, and overhead by Friday. \nThis pattern will mean highs on Thursday similar to today, \nmainly ranging from the low to mid 60s over the Turtle Mountains\nand vicinity, to the lower 70s across the southwest. Then as \nthe ridge axis moves overhead on Friday, a warm front will lift \nnorth across the forecast area. This feature should lead to well\nabove normal temperatures across most of the area on Friday \nwith highs ranging from the low to mid 70s northeast to the low \nto mid 80s southwest.\n\nWe then transition to southwest flow aloft on Saturday as an\nenergetic trough starts to amplify/dig over the Pacific\nNorthwest. A surface low will drag a potent but dry cold front \nacross the state from west to east at some point on Saturday \nwhile timing still remains a bit uncertain. Most of the forecast\narea should still see highs in the lower 70s to lower 80s but\nthe northwest may not climb out of the 60s as the front moves\nthrough here before peak heating. This frontal passage will lead\nto much colder temperatures Saturday night into Sunday for most\nlocations. Widespread lows in the mid to upper 30s are expected\nacross much of the north and west with readings mainly in the\nlow to mid 40s elsewhere. The one exception could be over the\nfar south central and James River Valley where a few upper 40s \nand lower 50s remain possible. Much will depend on the frontal \npassage timing here. Highs on Sunday will only range from the\nmid 40s northwest to the upper 50s/lower 60s southeast. The\ncoldest air of the season thus far then arrives Sunday\nnight/Monday morning with lows mainly in the 30s. A few readings\nin the upper 20s will even be possible across the northwest.\nFreeze headlines may end up being needed as we get closer.  \n\nPrecipitation chances will also start to increase Saturday \nnight/Sunday as a shortwave ejects into the northern Plains \nfrom the western trough. The flow then splits as an upper low \ncuts off across the southwest CONUS and another messy shortwave \nor two crosses the region in the northern stream Monday/Monday\nnight. This will lead to widespread medium/high chances of\nprecipitation at times Saturday night through Monday night.\nOverall QPF amounts don't appear to be too impressive at this\ntime with NBM 24-hour QPF probabilities of quarter inch or \ngreater maxing out in the 30 to 60 percent range. While the vast\nmajority of the precipitation will fall as rain, some snow may \nmix in across the west Sunday night/Monday morning. The highest \nprecipitation chances and highest amounts are favored across the\nsouthwest. \n\nModel agreement then breaks down towards midweek but progressive\nridging is favored to move through on Tuesday which may give us\nat least a little break in precipitation chances. Ensemble \nspread remains high during this time frame as the models \nstruggle to resolve the progression of the southwest CONUS upper\nlow, so will continue to run with the NBM slight chances of \nprecipitation on Wednesday. Temperatures will remain on the \ncolder side with more widespread readings in the 30s for lows \nTuesday and Wednesday mornings. Progressive ridging may lead to \na slight warmup for highs by midweek.",
